  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This scholarly book shows early but meaningful uptake: it has been cited twice, attracted 1,562 downloads through OAPEN, and is represented in library holdings across subject areas including philosophy, theology, anthropology, and colonialism &amp; imperialism. Its record also suggests policy- and sustainability-adjacent relevance through an SDG alignment with gender equality, while the funded open-access pathway supports broad availability.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Ernst Wolff’s book on Mongameli Mabona has already begun to circulate in scholarly and public-facing library ecosystems. The citation record is still small, but it is real: OpenAlex shows two citations, both appearing in 2021, including an article in the African Journal of Inter/Multidisciplinary Studies and a bibliography entry. That pattern points to an emerging scholarly footprint rather than a mature citation history, so the safest reading is that the book is beginning to enter academic conversations, especially around African cultural and philosophical studies and related humanities topics.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage evidence is stronger than citation volume at this stage. OAPEN reports 1,562 downloads, indicating sustained reading interest and practical reach for an open-access scholarly book. The monthly download pattern shows continued attention across the year rather than a single short-lived spike. Because downloads measure access and use rather than formal citation, they should be read as complementary evidence: the book is being consulted, even as its citation base is still developing.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dissemination profile also supports educational and interdisciplinary relevance. WorldCat and Open Library holdings place the book in collections associated with anthropology, theology, philosophy, colonialism &amp; imperialism, and biography, suggesting that it is discoverable by readers across humanities and social-science contexts. OpenAIRE records the work as openly accessible, including an openly available book instance, and the publication is linked to KU Leuven support through a Thoth-verified funding record. Together, these signals show a publication positioned for broad access and long-term scholarly visibility.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book’s thematic alignment with gender equality further indicates a policy- and sustainability-adjacent dimension, though that should be understood as an interpretive signal rather than a claim of direct policy use. No public event archive was available in the record, so there is no documented event-based discussion to add. Overall, the evidence portrays a book with clear early reach, open-access availability, and growing scholarly visibility, while the citation record remains modest and should be interpreted as an early-stage signal rather than a settled measure of impact.</p>",
